						    <h4 class="pt-20">Net Worth</h4>

						    <p>
						        Net worth is the total value of everything you own minus everything you owe.
						        It provides a snapshot of your overall financial health.
						    </p>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Understanding the Formula</h4>

						    <p>
						        <strong>Net Worth = Assets − Liabilities</strong>
						    </p>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Breaking Down the Components</h4>

						    <h4>Assets (What You Own)</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li>Cash in bank accounts</li>
						        <li>Investment and retirement accounts</li>
						        <li>Real estate (home value)</li>
						        <li>Vehicles</li>
						        <li>Valuable personal property (jewellery, artwork, etc.)</li>
						    </ul>

						    <h4>Liabilities (What You Owe)</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li>Home loans / Mortgages</li>
						        <li>Car loans</li>
						        <li>Education loans</li>
						        <li>Credit card outstanding balances</li>
						        <li>Personal loans</li>
						    </ul>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Why It Matters</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li><strong>Tracks Financial Progress:</strong> Shows whether your wealth is growing over time.</li>
						        <li><strong>Measures Financial Health:</strong> Provides a more complete picture than income alone.</li>
						        <li><strong>Supports Loan Applications:</strong> Helps lenders evaluate your financial stability.</li>
						    </ul>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Types of Net Worth</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li><strong>Positive Net Worth:</strong> Your assets are worth more than your liabilities.</li>
						        <li><strong>Negative Net Worth:</strong> Your liabilities exceed the value of your assets.</li>
						    </ul>

						    <hr>

						    <h3 class="pt-20">Budgeting and Cash Flow</h3>

						    <p>
						        A budget is a financial plan that tracks your income and expenses over a
						        specific period. It helps you manage your money, avoid unnecessary debt,
						        and save towards future financial goals.
						    </p>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Core Components</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li><strong>Income:</strong> Total money earned or received.</li>
						        <li><strong>Fixed Expenses:</strong> Regular expenses that remain mostly unchanged, such as rent or EMI.</li>
						        <li><strong>Variable Expenses:</strong> Expenses that vary each month, such as groceries, shopping, and entertainment.</li>
						        <li><strong>Savings:</strong> Money reserved for emergencies and future goals.</li>
						    </ul>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Main Purposes</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li><strong>Control Spending:</strong> Helps prevent unnecessary expenses.</li>
						        <li><strong>Track Progress:</strong> Shows exactly where your money is spent.</li>
						        <li><strong>Reach Financial Goals:</strong> Supports saving for emergencies, education, vacations, or retirement.</li>
						        <li><strong>Reduce Financial Stress:</strong> Improves confidence and financial security.</li>
						    </ul>

						    <h4 class="pt-20">Popular Budgeting Methods</h4>

						    <ul>
						        <li><strong>50/25/25 Rule:</strong> Allocate 50% to needs, 25% to wants, and 25% to savings.</li>
						        <li><strong>Zero-Based Budgeting:</strong> Every rupee is assigned a purpose until the balance reaches zero.</li>
						        <li><strong>Envelope System:</strong> Divide spending money into separate envelopes for different expense categories.</li>
						    </ul>

						    <hr>

						    <h3 class="pt-20">Know Your Liabilities</h3>

						    <p>
						        The phrase "know your liabilities" is a fundamental rule in business, law, and personal finance. It means understanding exactly what you owe, what you are responsible for, and where you are legally or financially vulnerable.  It also declares the overall outgo of money from your income and hole it leaves in your value
